{"parentUuid":"ea268b9c-ba28-42b7-b9b6-23b933d320b7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"88b6ccb8-7105-4de5-afa1-ccbae8a94e54","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DPRFevEAe7ADZ2VQRhhMHC","type":"tool_result","content":"60:## 4. Step 2 — assert the layout invariants\n61:\n62:| Key | Must be | If not |\n63:|---|---|---|\n64:| `tracked_symlinks_in_canonical` | `0` | RED — a link was committed; Windows gets a dead file |\n65:| `pool_view_drift`, `codex_view_drift` | `0` | RED — someone edited a generated view instead of `~/.claude/skills/` |\n66:| `pool_dangling`, `codex_dangling` | `0` | RED — a deleted canonical skill left an abandoned link |\n67:| `portability_failures` | `0` | RED — see the parity check in Step 4 |\n68:\n69:Information, never findings: `third_party_links_in_canonical` (changes with every\n70:install), `pool_third_party`, `codex_third_party` (deliberately preserved installs).\n71:\n72:## 5. Step 3 — delegate under the contract\n73:\n74:For each due layer, invoke the owner named in `rot.md`, **read-only**:\n75:\n76:- Say it plainly in the invocation: *report findings only; apply nothing, write no\n77:  ledger, create no files.*\n78:- `improve-system` applies previously approved proposals in its own Step 0 —\n79:  suppress that explicitly. `brain-audit` appends to its own trend table — suppress\n80:  that too, or leave the Vault layer `unknown`.\n81:- Name the scope you need. Fold the delegate's findings under that layer's heading.\n82:- A receipt covers only what was checked. If the delegate covered less than its\n83:  row claims, write the date **with a scope note**, or leave the row `unknown`.\n84:  Never stamp a date because a delegate exited cleanly.\n85:\n86:The Memory (agent) layer has no delegate — audit it directly: every `MEMORY.md`\n87:index line resolves to a file; every memory file appears in its index;\n88:`~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` is under 2,500 chars; flag time-dimensioned\n89:facts (counters, \"still open\", past-tense futures).\n90:\n91:## 6. Step 4 — the harness layer, audited directly\n92:\n93:- **Bloat.** List every canonical skill never invoked across all sessions\n94:  (`never_used` from the metrics; name them).\n95:- **Crutch test.** For each never-used and once-used skill, answer the `rot.md`\n96:  question — *would the current model do this fine without it?* Recommend\n97:  DELETE, KEEP (tacit knowledge the model cannot know), or SEASONAL. One line of\n98:  reasoning each.\n99:- **Overlap.** Skills whose descriptions claim the same trigger space. Recommend\n100:  a merge, or a sharper `Do not use … (use X)` clause on both.\n101:- **Parity.** Run `python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`;\n102:  every failure is a finding. Then check config-level parity, which the linter\n103:  cannot see: list canonical skills set `\"off\"` in `~/.claude/settings.json`\n104:  `skillOverrides`. Those are hidden in Claude Code but live in Codex and Pi.\n105:  Each one needs a decision: switch it back on, or retire it from canonical.\n106:- **Promotion.** Global skills used in fewer than 2 projects — demotion\n107:  candidates. Project skills used in 2+ projects — promotion candidates.\n108:- **Rules.** Diff `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` against the last 7 days of sessions.\n109:  Report rules contradicted by what actually happened, and corrections the user\n110:  repeated twice that are not yet rules.\n111:- **Hooks.** Compare `hooks_claude` with `hooks_codex`, then diff the two\n112:  directories by name. The copies must match (patch both, always).\n113:- **Agents.** If `agents=0`, report \"no agents defined\" and skip. Do not invent a finding.\n114:\n115:## 7. Step 5 — write the report\n116:\n117:Write `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md` with one section per\n118:layer (green-skipped layers get one line). Update `Last audited` in\n119:`~/.claude/rot.md` **only for layers whose check actually ran and produced\n120:output**, then `cp ~/.claude/rot.md ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/rot.md`.\n121:\n122:Do **not** write to `docs/audits/aios-trend.md` — `scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh` is\n123:its single writer. If the cron left an uncommitted trend row, commit it together\n124:with this report.\n125:\n126:## 8. Step 6 — end with exactly two proposed next builds\n127:\n128:Ranked. Each with the one command or sentence that starts it. Not ten. Two.\n129:\n130:## Examples\n131:\n132:**Monthly nudge**\n133:```\n134:User: \"audit my AI OS\"\n135:→ reads rot.md: Skills and Rules past due, Hooks inside its window\n136:→ metrics: all invariants 0; never_used=19\n137:→ delegates nothing for Skills/Rules (owned here); skips Hooks green\n138:→ report with crutch-test verdicts, 2 parity decisions, 2 next builds\n139:```\n140:\n141:**One layer**\n142:```\n143:User: \"/aios-audit Memory (agent)\"\n144:→ audits only the agent-memory stores directly\n145:→ stamps that one row in rot.md; every other row untouched\n146:```\n147:\n148:## Gotchas\n149:\n150:- **Linter green is not parity.** `skill-portability-check.py` reads frontmatter\n151:  only. A skill set `\"off\"` in `skillOverrides` passes it and still never fires in\n152:  Claude Code, while Codex (which ignores that file) loads it. Found 2026-09-12:\n153:  12 canonical skills off in Claude, live in Codex.\n154:- **Views are links into canonical.** Anything that writes into\n155:  `~/.agents/skills/<owned>/` by path writes canonical. `graphify install\n156:  --platform agents` does exactly that and overwrites the Claude variant — upgrade\n157:  graphify with `--platform claude` only.\n158:- **Concurrent sessions commit your work.** `closeout` in another session sweeps\n159:  every uncommitted change in the repos it visits. Commit the report as soon as it\n160:  is written, not at the end of a long session.\n161:- **Description length has a budget.** Claude Code caps the combined skill\n162:  description size; very long descriptions risk a skill silently dropping out of\n163:  the listing. When recommending a sharper `Do not use` clause, keep the whole\n164:  description near 650 characters.\n165:- **`dcg` blocks some shell shapes** (`rm -rf` inside heredocs, `$VAR` redirects,\n166:  `git` inside command substitution). Use Python `subprocess` or literal paths;\n167:  do not try to bypass the guard.\n---\n/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/gateC-keystone/claudex-cd553k48/result.json\n{'verdict': 'REVISE', 'summary': 'Three concrete Gate C defects remain: the scheduled entry point loses executability in Git, stale-lock recovery permits concurrent writers, and Claude-only usage counts are presented as cross-harness non-use.', 'findings': [{'id': 'C-01', 'severity': 'medium', 'path': 'scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh', 'evidence': \"Git records this script as mode 100644, although the working copy is executable. The installed launchd plist runs `/bin/bash -lc '$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh'`, which executes the script directly. A fresh checkout therefore fails with permission denied; the current host's successful run depends on an unversioned permission change.\", 'fix': 'Commit executable mode 100755, or change the plist to pass the script as an explicit argument to /bin/bash.'}, {'id': 'C-02', 'severity': 'medium', 'path': 'scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh:21', 'evidence': \"Stale-lock reclamation is not atomic. Two invocations can both read the same dead owner. After the first removes and recreates the lock, the second executes its already-decided `rm -rf`, deleting the first invocation's live lock, then acquires its own. Both append trend rows, and either EXIT trap can remove the other's lock. The supplied sequential live/stale checks do not cover this interleaving.\", 'fix': 'Serialize reclamation with a separate atomic recovery lock, recheck ownership while holding it, and release locks only when their ownership token still belongs to the releasing process.'}, {'id': 'C-03', 'severity': 'medium', 'path': 'scripts/aios-metrics.py:46', 'evidence': \"`usage()` reads only ~/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl, silently ignores read failures, and `never_used` subtracts those results from the entire owned roster. The audit then treats these as skills never invoked 'across all sessions' and uses them for DELETE recommendations. A skill used exclusively in Codex or Pi—or whose Claude transcript is unreadable—is incorrectly classified as never used.\", 'fix': 'Label this metric explicitly as observed Claude usage, report unavailable transcript evidence, and prohibit cross-harness non-use conclusions from it. Alternatively, incorporate supported Codex/Pi usage evidence before computing the combined metric.'}], 'coverage': ['Read the supplied plan and confirmed its on-disk SHA256 matches the requested hash.', 'Inspected all seven manifested files, the installed launchd plist, and the live rot.md.', 'Traced the portability-checker caller, sync manifest writers, daily-backup error-log writer, and session-start error-log reader.', \"Compared Git's recorded script mode with working-copy permissions and launchd invocation.\"], 'limitations': ['Static, read-only review; no tests, scheduled jobs, edits, or delegation performed.', 'Host-reported verification was treated as evidence, not independently reproduced.', 'Windows filesystem behavior and Pi runtime behavior were not exercised.', 'The known seven-skill roster decision remains a separate unresolved Gate B item; this review does not approve overall harness parity.']}","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"150e809a-d6cd-4e35-be18-332c60ad4dd4","timestamp":"2026-09-13T06:05:20.332Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"60:## 4. Step 2 — assert the layout invariants\n61:\n62:| Key | Must be | If not |\n63:|---|---|---|\n64:| `tracked_symlinks_in_canonical` | `0` | RED — a link was committed; Windows gets a dead file |\n65:| `pool_view_drift`, `codex_view_drift` | `0` | RED — someone edited a generated view instead of `~/.claude/skills/` |\n66:| `pool_dangling`, `cod