{"parentUuid":"93c7b655-94ae-4289-beb8-4f9f6b5239b5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"edac4314-7853-44b7-9152-71a2296efc96","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017WHtgaaaZFw5yGG6FjYH55","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nname: aios-audit\ndescription: >-\n  Audit the whole AI operating system across decay tiers - skills, agents,\n  hooks, rules, memory, and cross-harness parity between Claude Code, Codex,\n  and Pi - then propose exactly two next builds. Use when the user says\n  \"/aios-audit\", \"audit my system\", \"audit my AI OS\", \"audit the bloat\",\n  \"is my setup rotting\", \"what should I cut\", \"are my skills portable\",\n  \"monthly skill audit\", or when rot.md shows a layer past its audit date.\n  Do not use for project data drift, routing, or index freshness - use\n  os-audit. Do not use for launchd, venv, or backup health - use\n  claude-stack-doctor. Do not use for SecondBrain note rot - use\n  improve-system or brain-audit.\nmetadata:\n  short-description: Decay-tier audit of the AI OS; delegates to the five layer-specific audit skills.\nargument-hint: \"[optional: a layer name from rot.md]\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Agent\n---\n\n# AIOS Audit — is the harness itself still true?\n\nFive audit skills already exist, and each owns one layer. This skill owns the\nlayer none of them touch: **the harness** — skill roster, agents, hooks, rules,\nagent memory, and parity between Claude Code, Codex, and Pi. For every other\nlayer it **delegates**; it never re-implements another audit.\n\n| Delegate | Owns |\n|---|---|\n| `os-audit` (project) | project data: routing, index truth, freshness, duplication |\n| `brain-audit` | SecondBrain as a system — the four C's |\n| `improve-system` | note-level rot: stale seeds, duplicates, conflicts |\n| `claude-stack-doctor` | automation plumbing: launchd, venvs, memsearch, backup freshness |\n| `doctor-plus` | context-engineering posture of the workspace |\n\nIf `$ARGUMENTS` names one layer from `rot.md`, audit only that layer.\n\n## 1. Read-only\n\nThe only writes are the report file and the `Last audited` column of\n`~/.claude/rot.md`. **Never delete, archive, disable, or edit a skill during the\naudit.** Propose, then wait for approval. A deletion proposal that turns out wrong\nis cheap; a deletion is not.\n\n## 2. Step 0 — read `~/.claude/rot.md`\n\nFor each layer, compute days since `Last audited`. A layer that is **past due or\n`unknown`** is audited this run. A layer inside its window is reported green and\nskipped — that is what keeps a monthly run cheap. Read the delegation contract and\nthe layout invariants in `rot.md` before going further; they bind every step below.\n\n## 3. Step 1 — run the metrics\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n```\n\nCapture every `key=value` line. Any stack trace, or a value like `git_error`,\n`no_manifest` or `error_rc…`, is itself a RED finding — do not guess the number.\n\n----- ROT -----\n# rot.md — how fast each AIOS layer goes stale\n\nAudit each layer on its own clock, not all at once. `aios-audit` reads this\nfile: any layer past due, or marked `unknown`, is a finding.\n\n**A date here is a receipt, not an intention.** Write one only after that\nlayer's check in the last column has actually run and produced output.\n\n| Layer | Where it lives | Rots | Audit every | Last audited | Owner | Concrete check |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Identity | `global-memory/USER.md` | very slow | 3 months | unknown | memory-write | Read USER.md; flag any role, family, or career fact contradicted by the last 90 days of sessions. |\n| Substrate | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `~/DEV/*.md` routers | slow | 3 months | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 1 (routing integrity). |\n| Skills | `~/.claude/skills/` | fast | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit | `skill-portability-check.py` + never-used list + the crutch test below. |\n| Agents | `~/.claude/agents/` | fast | 1 month | unknown | aios-audit | Count `*.md`. Currently **0 exist** — report \"no agents defined\" and skip until one does. |\n| Rules | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` behaviour rules, output styles | fastest | 1 week | unknown | aios-audit | Diff CLAUDE.md against the last 7 days of sessions: any rule contradicted by what actually happened, and any correction the user repeated twice that is not yet a rule. |\n| Hooks | `~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/` | slow | 6 months | unknown | claude-stack-doctor | Delegate. Also diff the two copies — they must match (`hook-two-copies-trap`). |\n| Memory (agent) | `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `global-memory/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | **aios-audit itself** | Audited directly — see the delegation contract below. `improve-system` does NOT cover these stores. Check: every `MEMORY.md` index line resolves to a file; every memory file appears in its index; `global-memory/MEMORY.md` is under its 2,500-char cap; flag time-dimensioned facts (counters, \"still open\", past-tense futures). |\n| Memory (vault notes) | `~/SecondBrain/` notes | medium | 1 month | unknown | improve-system | Delegate — but read-only, per the contract below. |\n| Data feeds | Fireflies, GPS, CFBD, Zotero | varies | 1 week | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 3 (freshness). |\n| Vault | `~/SecondBrain/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | brain-audit | Delegate to `brain-audit` (four C's score). |\n| Context posture | workspace-wide | medium | 3 months | unknown | doctor-plus | Delegate to `doctor-plus`. |\n\n## The question that governs a skill's fate\n\nNot \"how often did I run it?\" but **\"would the current model do this fine\nwithout the skill?\"** If yes, the skill is a crutch that now limits the model.\nDelete it. Keep only skills carrying tacit knowledge the model cannot know:\nforce-plate thresholds, GPS conventions, Baylor reporting norms, Eric's own\nroutines.\n\nSeasonal skills (`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`) are exempt from low-use\npressure. Judge them in season.\n\n## Delegation contract (R2-09)\n\nA delegate is invoked **read-only**, for a **named scope**, and its receipt is\nwritten **only** for the scope it actually covered.\n\n1. **Read-only.** Before invoking a delegate, instruct it explicitly: report\n   findings, apply nothing, write no ledger, create no files. Several delegates\n   (`improve-system` among them) begin by applying previously approved proposals\n   — that must be suppressed. `aios-audit` writes only its own report and the\n   `Last audited` column.\n2. **Scope must be verified, not assumed.** A delegate's actual coverage is what\n   its body scans, not what this table wishes it scanned. `improve-system` scans\n   `~/SecondBrain/` — Notes, Knowledge, Projects. It does **not** touch\n   `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/` or `global-memory/`. Those are audited by\n   `aios-audit` directly.\n3. **A receipt covers only what was checked.** If a delegate returns findings for\n   a narrower scope than its row claims, write the date **and** a scope note, or\n   leave the row `unknown`. Never stamp a date because a delegate exited cleanly.\n\n## Layout invariants the audit must assert\n\n- `~/.claude/skills/` is canonical, is a git repo, and contains **no TRACKED symlinks**\n  (`git ls-files -s` shows no mode 120000). On-disk links to third-party skills are\n  expected; they are listed in the per-machine `.git/info/exclude` (never the tracked\n  `.gitignore`), and a pre-commit hook refuses to commit one. What must never happen is\n  committing a link, because Windows pulls this repo and a Mac link is dead there.\n- `~/.agents/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` are **generated views**. Each carries\n  a `.generated-by-sync-to-codex` manifest. Every entry **in the manifest** must be\n  a symlink on Mac (a copy on Windows); wrong representation is drift.\n- Entries **not** in the manifest are third-party installs (`npx skills add`).\n  They are counted and reported, **never** flagged as errors and never pruned.\n- Support directories (`_shared/`) have no SKILL.md and must never be pruned.\n\n## Direction of travel\n\n**Fewer skills, stricter rules.** Skills get deleted; rules get sharpened.\n\n## Cadence\n\nMonthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 14:00.\nIt writes a trend row and a nudge. It does not commit and does not push.\nThe agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e433fe5d-1e9e-46cd-a27d-b4e49d9deafa","timestamp":"2026-09-13T06:25:27.236Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nname: aios-audit\ndescription: >-\n  Audit the whole AI operating system across decay tiers - skills, agents,\n  hooks, rules, memory, and cross-harness parity between Claude Code, Codex,\n  and Pi - then propose exactly two next builds. Use when the user says\n  \"/aios-audit\", \"audit my system\", \"audit my AI OS\", \"audit the bloat\",\n  \"is my setup rotting\", \"what should I cut\", \"are my skills portable\",\n  \"monthly skill audit\", or when rot.md shows a layer past its audit date.\n  Do not use for project data drift, routing, or index freshness - use\n  os-audit. Do not use for launchd, venv, or backup health - use\n  claude-stack-doctor. Do not use for SecondBrain note rot - use\n  improve-system or brain-audit.\nmetadata:\n  short-description: Decay-tier audit of the AI OS; delegates to the five layer-specific audit skills.\nargument-hint: \"[optional: a layer name from rot.md]\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Agent\n---\n\n# AIOS Audit — is the harness itself still true?\n\nFive audit skills already exist, and each owns one layer. This skill owns the\nlayer none of them touch: **the harness** — skill roster, agents, hooks, rules,\nagent memory, and parity between Claude Code, Codex, and Pi. For every other\nlayer it **delegates**; it never re-implements another audit.\n\n| Delegate | Owns |\n|---|---|\n| `os-audit` (project) | project data: routing, index truth, freshness, duplication |\n| `brain-audit` | SecondBrain as a system — the four C's |\n| `improve-system` | note-level rot: stale seeds, duplicates, conflicts |\n| `claude-stack-doctor` | automation plumbing: launchd, venvs, memsearch, backup freshness |\n| `doctor-plus` | context-engineering posture of the workspace |\n\nIf `$ARGUMENTS` names one layer from `rot.md`, audit only that layer.\n\n## 1. Read-only\n\nThe only writes are the report file and the `Last audited` column of\n`~/.claude/rot.md`. **Never delete, archive, disable, or edit a skill during the\naudit.** Propose, then wait for approval. A deletion proposal that turns out wrong\nis cheap; a deletion is not.\n\n## 2. Step 0 — read `~/.claude/rot.md`\n\nFor each layer, compute days since `Last audited`. A layer that is **past due or\n`unknown`** is audited this run. A layer inside its window is reported green and\nskipped — that is what keeps a monthly run cheap. Read the delegation contract and\nthe layout invariants in `rot.md` before going further; they bind every step below.\n\n## 3. Step 1 — run the metrics\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n```\n\nCapture every `key=value` line. Any stack trace, or a value like `git_error`,\n`no_manifest` or `error_rc…`, is itself a RED finding — do not guess the number.\n\n----- ROT -----\n# rot.md — how fast each AIOS layer goes stale\n\nAudit each layer on its own clock, not all at once. `aios-audit` reads this\nfile: any layer past due, or marked `unknown`, is a finding.\n\n**A date here is a receipt, not an intention.** Write one only after that\nlayer's check in the last column has actually run and produced output.\n\n| Layer | Where it lives | Rots | Audit every | Last audited | Owner | Concrete check |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Identity | `global-memory/USER.md` | very slow | 3 months | unknown | memory-write | Read USER.md; flag any role, family, or career fact contradicted by the last 90 days of sessions. |\n| Substrate | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `~/DEV/*.md` routers | slow | 3 months | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 1 (routing integrity). |\n| Skills | `~/.claude/skills/` | fast | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit | `skill-portability-check.py` + never-used list + the crutch test below. |\n| Agents | `~/.claude/agents/` | fast | 1 month | unknown | aios-audit | Count `*.md`. Currently **0 exist** — report \"no agents defined\" and skip until one does. |\n| Rules | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` behaviour rules, output styles | fastest | 1 week | unknown | aios-audit | Diff CLAUDE.md against the last 7 days of sessions: any rule contradicted by what actually happened, and any correction the user repeated twice that is not yet a rule. |\n| Hooks | `~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/` | slow | 6 months | unknown | claude-stack-doctor | Delegate. Also diff the two copies — they must match (`hook-two-copies-trap`). |\n| Memory (agent) | `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `global-memory/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | **aios-audit itself** | Audited directly — see the delegation contract below. `improve-system` does NOT cover these stores. Check: every `MEMORY.md` index line resolves to a file; every memory file appears in its index; `global-memory/MEMORY.md` is under its 2,500-char cap; flag time-dimensioned facts (counters, \"still open\", past-tense futures). |\n| Memory (vault notes) | `~/SecondBrain/` notes | medium | 1 month | unknown | improve-system | Delegate — but read-only, per the contract below. |\n| Data feeds | Fireflies, GPS, CFBD, Zotero | varies | 1 week | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 3 (freshness). |\n| Vault | `~/SecondBrain/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | brain-audit | Delegate to `brain-audit` (four C's score). |\n| Context posture | workspace-wide | medium | 3 months | unknown | doctor-plus | Delegate to `doctor-plus`. |\n\n## The question that governs a skill's fate\n\nNot \"how often did I run it?\" but **\"would the current model do this fine\nwithout the skill?\"** If yes, the skill is a crutch that now limits the model.\nDelete it. Keep only skills carrying tacit knowledge the model cannot know:\nforce-plate thresholds, GPS conventions, Baylor reporting norms, Eric's own\nroutines.\n\nSeasonal skills (`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`) are exempt from low-use\npressure. Judge them in season.\n\n## Delegation contract (R2-09)\n\nA delegate is invoked **read-only**, for a **named scope**, and its receipt is\nwritten **only** for the scope it actually covered.\n\n1. **Read-only.** Before invoking a delegate, instruct it explicitly: report\n   findings, apply nothing, write no ledger, create no files. Several delegates\n   (`improve-system` among them) begin by applying previously approved proposals\n   — that must be suppressed. `aios-audit` writes only its own report and the\n   `Last audited` column.\n2. **Scope must be verified, not assumed.** A delegate's actual coverage is what\n   its body scans, not what this table wishes it scanned. `improve-system` scans\n   `~/SecondBrain/` — Notes, Knowledge, Projects. It does **not** touch\n   `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/` or `global-memory/`. Those are audited by\n   `aios-audit` directly.\n3. **A receipt covers only what was checked.** If a delegate returns findings for\n   a narrower scope than its row claims, write the date **and** a scope note, or\n   leave the row `unknown`. Never stamp a date because a delegate exited cleanly.\n\n## Layout invariants the audit must assert\n\n- `~/.claude/skills/` is canonical, is a git repo, and contains **no TRACKED symlinks**\n  (`git ls-files -s` shows no mode 120000). On-disk links to third-party skills are\n  expected; they are listed in the per-machine `.git/info/exclude` (never the tracked\n  `.gitignore`), and a pre-commit hook refuses to commit one. What must never happen is\n  committing a link, because Windows pulls this repo and a Mac link is dead there.\n- `~/.agents/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` are **generated views**. Each carries\n  a `.generated-by-sync-to-codex` manifest. Every entry **in the manifest** must be\n  a symlink on Mac (a copy on Windows); wrong representation is drift.\n- Entries **not** in the manifest are third-party installs (`npx skills add`).\n  They are counted and reported, **never** flagged as errors and never pruned.\n- Support directories (`_shared/`) have no SKILL.md and must never be pruned.\n\n## Direction of travel\n\n**Fewer skills, stricter rules.** Skills get deleted; rules get sharpened.\n\n## Cadence\n\nMonthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 14:00.\nIt writes a trend row and a nudge. It does not commit and does not push.\nThe agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"93c7b655-94ae-4289-beb8-4f9f6b5239b5","session_id":"9823c1f0-89f5-4638-9223-c6892607ac2b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"9823c1f0-89f5-4638-9223-c6892607ac2b","version":"2.1.270","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"37ac2c52-952d-40fd-8cae-2d3fbc8a0899","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"edac4314-7853-44b7-9152-71a2296efc96","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WGvZGwQuMPCZRjByTaudak","type":"tool_result","content":"codex-cli 0.154.0\n2:model = \"gpt-5.6-sol\"\n3:model_reasoning_effort = \"high\"\n4:model_verbosity = \"low\"\n3:description: \"Recommend a model and a scoped handoff for a task in Claude Code or Codex. Use when choosing who should handle a task, seeking a second opinion, getting unstuck, or delegating focused work; execute one handoff when requested.\"\n12:Use the current conversation and just enough relevant project context to identify the host, current model when known, desired outcome, and constraints. Consider ambiguity, code dependencies, verification difficulty, context size, and cost or speed preferences. A short prompt can describe a difficult task. Preserve explicit model and provider choices; do not silently replace them with your preferred pairing.\n16:## Choose the role before the model\n24:| A separable task has clear inputs and acceptance checks | Delegate that piece to a suitable smaller model and inspect its result |\n31:Start with models available in the user's environment. The following are dated starting points (September 2026), not a permanent leaderboard:\n33:- **GPT-5.6 Luna** (`gpt-5.6-luna`): consider for narrow, repetitive tasks with explicit checks, such as fixtures, extraction, documentation updates, or an isolated helper.\n34:- **GPT-5.6 Terra** (`gpt-5.6-terra`): consider for bounded coding or investigation requiring more judgment and context than the Luna task above.\n35:- **GPT-6 Astra** (`gpt-6-astra`) and **Claude Fable 5.1** (`claude-fable-5-1`): candidates for ambiguous work, difficult debugging, or a substantial independent review. From Codex, Fable is a candidate for cross-provider review; from Claude Code, Astra is a candidate.\n36:- **Claude Sonnet, Opus, or another available model**: retain as options when their task fit, existing context, account access, or the user's preference favors them. Cross-provider delegation is optional.\n38:Use local model listings and CLI status/help when accessible without launching a model task. Distinguish listed, authenticated, and proven runnable: none alone establishes the others. If access or the active model is unknown, make the recommendation conditional and explain what needs checking. Do not launch paid comparison calls just to choose a model.\n40:For price-sensitive choices or comparative claims, consult current official [OpenAI model information](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models) and [Anthropic model information](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview). Check [Codex usage guidance](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/pricing) when using subscription allowances. API token prices are different from subscription usage; task costs also include context transfer, reasoning, retries, and host verification. Do not call Terra cheaper than Sonnet, claim Luna is universally stronger, or promise savings without relevant evidence. If sources cannot be checked, omit numeric claims and label the cost assumption.\n46:- **Recommendation:** stay here or use a named provider/model for a specific role.\n50:Offer at most one alternative when it helps a real tradeoff. Do not interview the user, generate a catalog of models, or start a plan-review loop. Do not claim to have changed the current session's model; a child CLI invocation is a separate session.\n54:Use the selected provider's CLI from the correct project directory, explicitly selecting the recommended or requested model for that call. Check the actual binary's version and help; consult the relevant official [Codex non-interactive guide](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/non-interactive-mode) or [Claude programmatic guide](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/headless) if needed. Avoid changing global defaults, installing software, or switching models silently to make a call succeed.\n60:Use a fresh session for the one-off handoff, a bounded timeout, and separate stdout/stderr artifacts in a unique temporary directory outside the project. Keep the user informed during long calls. Read the completed result and exit status; an empty response, timeout, or permission failure is not success. Stop and report a failed handoff rather than automatically retrying, escalating to a larger model, or starting another round. If a timed-out process may still run, resolve its status before restarting work on the same files.\n62:Assess findings against evidence, inspect any edits, and run appropriate checks within existing authorization. Report the outcome, checks actually run, and remaining uncertainty. Distinguish the model requested from the model observed; do not invent an observed identity. If a correction is outside the requested work, recommend it without expanding scope. Finish after this handoff and host verification; further delegation follows the user's request.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f0d4f3ba-5cbd-410d-b53d-f3d3077f5eb6","timestamp":"2026-09-13T06:25:29.731Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"codex-cli 0.154.0\n2:model = \"gpt-5.6-sol\"\n3:model_reasoning_effort = \"high\"\n4:model_verbosity = \"low\"\n3:description: \"Recommend a model and a scoped handoff for a task in Claude Code or Codex. Use when choosing who should handle a task, seeking a second opinion, getting unstuck, or delegating focused work; execute one handoff when requested.\"\n12:Use the current conversation and just enough relevant project context to identify the host, current model when known, desired outcome, and constraints. Consider ambiguity, code dependencies, verification difficulty, context size, and cost or speed preferences. A short prompt can describe a difficult task. Preserve explicit model and provider choices; do not silently replace them with your preferred pairing.\n16:## Choose the role before the model\n24:| A separable task has clear inputs and acceptance checks | Delegate that piece to a suitable smaller model and inspect its result |\n31:Start with models available in the user's environment. The following are dated starting points (September 2026), not a permanent leaderboard:\n33:- **GPT-5.6 Luna** (`gpt-5.6-luna`): consider for narrow, repetitive tasks with explicit checks, such as fixtures, extraction, documentation updates, or an isolated helper.\n34:- **GPT-5.6 Terra** (`gpt-5.6-terra`): consider for bounded coding or investigation requiring more judgment and context than the Luna task above.\n35:- **GPT-6 Astra** (`gpt-6-astra`) and **Claude Fable 5.1** (`claude-fable-5-1`): candidates for ambiguous work, difficult debugging, or a substantial independent review. From Codex, Fable is a candidate for cross-provider review; from Claude Code, Astra is a candidate.\n36:- **Claude Sonnet, Opus, or another available model**: retain as options when their task fit, existing context, account access, or the user's preference favors them. Cross-provider delegation is optional.\n38:Use local model listings and CLI status/help when accessible without launching a model task. Distinguish listed, authenticated, and proven runnable: none alone establishes the others. If access or the active model is unknown, make the recommendation conditional and explain what needs checking. Do not launch paid comparison calls just to choose a model.\n40:For price-sensitive choices or comparative claims, consult current official [OpenAI model information](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models) and [Anthropic model information](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview). Check [Codex usage guidance](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/pricing) when using subscription allowances. API token prices are different from subscription usage; task costs also include context transfer, reasoning, retries, and host verification. Do not call Terra cheaper than Sonnet, claim Luna is universally stronger, or promise savings without relevant evidence. If sources cannot be checked, omit numeric claims and label the cost assumption.\n46:- **Recommendation:** stay here or use a named provider/model for a specific role.\n50:Offer at most one alternative when it helps a real tradeoff. Do not interview the user, generate a catalog of models, or start a plan-review loop. Do not claim to have changed the current session's model; a child CLI invocation is a separate session.\n54:Use the selected provider's CLI from the correct project directory, explicitly selecting the recommended or requested model for that call. Check the actual binary's version and help; consult the relevant official [Codex non-interactive guide](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/non-interactive-mode) or [Claude programmatic guide](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/headless) if needed. Avoid changing global defaults, installing software, or switching models silently to make a call succeed.\n60:Use a fresh session for the one-off handoff, a bounded timeout, and separate stdout/stderr artifacts in a unique temporary directory outside the project. Keep the user informed during long calls. Read the completed result and exit status; an empty response, timeout, or permission failure is not success. Stop and report a failed handoff rather than automatically retrying, escalating to a larger model, or starting another round. If a timed-out process may still run, resolve its status before restarting work on the same files.\n62:Assess findings against evidence, inspect any edits, and run appropriate checks within existing authorization. Report the outcome, checks actually run, and remaining uncertainty. Distinguish the model requested from the model observed; do not invent an observed identity. If a correction is outside the requested work, recommend it without expanding scope. Finish after this handoff and host verification; further delegation follows the user's request.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"