{"parentUuid":"9c83c927-0d14-41da-a341-3d9989089321","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b4c0f99f-d416-4b0a-819d-31601dd3681d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fh6htLg41FgUMtQ6cSBR9c","type":"tool_result","content":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/9823c1f0/tmp/gateC4-keystone/claudex-5puly29v/result.json\nstatus: completed | usage: {'input_tokens': 881487, 'cached_input_tokens': 774912, 'cache_write_input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 8120, 'reasoning_output_tokens': 6297} | secs: 164.69\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: The implicit-type regexes over-classify valid YAML strings, so the checker can reject portable skill names.\n- IR-04 medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:190-193\n  EVIDENCE: The number regex accepts any digit sequence via `[0-9][0-9_]*`, so `name: 0008` is classified as a YAML number. PyYAML's YAML 1.1 resolver treats `0008` as a string (its octal branch rejects `8` and decimal branch disallows leading zero); YAML 1.2 core also does not resolve it as a number. A skill directory named `0008` with this valid string-valued name is therefore rejected as \"not a string.\" The added fixtures cover `123` and `1.5`, but not leading-zero or sexagesimal edge cases.\n  FIX: Model the YAML 1.1 and 1.2 numeric resolvers precisely and classify as non-string only if either exact grammar matches. Add string-control fixtures such as `0008`, `0129`, and `0:1`.\n- IR-05 medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:194-196\n  EVIDENCE: The timestamp regex permits unpadded date-only values such as `2026-9-3` because both month and day use `{1,2}`. PyYAML only resolves a date-only timestamp with two-digit month and day; its one-digit form is permitted only in the date-time alternative. YAML 1.2 core likewise requires `YYYY-MM-DD` for date-only timestamps. Thus a valid string skill name `2026-9-3` is falsely rejected as a timestamp.\n  FIX: Split date-only and date-time timestamp forms: require two-digit month/day for date-only values, whi