{"parentUuid":"6400d2ab-c000-4a50-8538-af543151ef4c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"1b4fec1f-d248-4f14-afc0-516ab3225ebd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UGM6mk8PcPVdnitmAW15dg","type":"tool_result","content":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/9823c1f0/tmp/gateC5-keystone/claudex-e6t18vyc/result.json\nstatus: failed | secs: 102.85 | out tok: 4820\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: Two YAML 1.1 false negatives remain in the final implicit-scalar grammar.\n- IR-06 medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:203\n  EVIDENCE: The stated contract rejects a value if either the PyYAML/YAML-1.1 or YAML-1.2-core resolver makes it non-string. The new timestamp regex treats `2026-9-3` as a scalar, but the supplied disposition/evidence identifies PyYAML’s result for that exact value as `timestamp`. Thus `name: 2026-9-3` in a same-named directory can pass type validation even though one supported resolver returns a timestamp. The corpus script includes this value and defines such a case as a false negative, so its reported zero-false-negative result is internally inconsistent with the current grammar.\n  FIX: Restore the YAML-1.1 date-only branch with one- or two-digit month/day when enforcing the union policy, and add an explicit fixture asserting `2026-9-3` is a timestamp. Alternatively, formally narrow the contract to YAML 1.2 only and remove the PyYAML-union claim and checks.\n- IR-07 medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:198-202\n  EVIDENCE: The rewritten YAML-1.1 float branches recognize exponents only after a decimal point. They omit the PyYAML float form `[-+]?[0-9][0-9_]*(?:[eE][-+][0-9]+)`. Consequently `name: 1_0e+3` is returned as `scalar`: the core branch cannot cover it because it disallows underscores, while PyYAML resolves it as a float. A directory named `1_0e+3` therefore passes despite the union contract. The pre-62fbb72 pattern covered this form through its decimal/exponent branch, so this is a regression.\n  FIX: Add a distinct YAML-1.1 exponent-only float alternative, e.g. `|[-+]?[0-9][0-9_]*(?:[eE][-+][0-9]+)`, and add `1_0e+3` and signed/exponent variants to the corpus and wrong-type fixtures.\nCOVERAGE: ['Static review of commit 62fbb72’s checker diff, final implicit-type code, Gate-C evidence scripts, audit disposition, status update, and change-manifest files.', 'Checked the requested false-positive/false-negative resolver boundary; no tests were run.']\nLIMITS: ['Read-only inspection only; evidence scripts and external YAML parsers were not executed.', 'Review was scoped to the final 62fbb72 change, as requested.']\n\n[exited with code 0]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d0296da2-3eb1-4b90-bae3-7b0fcb3b3e24","timestamp":"2026-09-13T06:55:12.753Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/9823c1f0/tmp/gateC5-keystone/claudex-e6t18vyc/result.json\nstatus: failed | secs: 102.85 | out tok: 4820\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: Two YAML 1.1 false negatives remain in the final implicit-scalar grammar.\n- IR-06 medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:203\n  EVIDENCE: The stated contract rejects a value if either the PyYAML/YAML-1.1 or YAML-1.2-core resolver makes it non-string. The new timestamp regex treats `2026-9-3` as a scalar, but the supplied disposition/evidence identifies PyYAML’s result for that exact value as `timestamp`. Thus `name: 2026-9-3` in a same-named directory can pass type validation even though one supported resolver returns a timestamp. The corpus script includes this value and defines such a case as a false negative, so its reported zero-false-negative result is internally inconsistent with the current grammar.\n  FIX: Restore the YAML-1.1 date-only branch with one- or two-digit month/day when enforcing the union policy, and add an explicit fixture asserting `2026-9-3` is a timestamp. Alternatively, formally narrow the contract to YAML 1.2 only and remove the PyYAML-union claim and checks.\n- IR-07 medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:198-202\n  EVIDENCE: The rewritten YAML-1.1 float branches recognize exponents only after a decimal point. They omit the PyYAML float form `[-+]?[0-9][0-9_]*(?:[eE][-+][0-9]+)`. Consequently `name: 1_0e+3` is returned as `scalar`: the core branch cannot cover it because it disallows underscores, while PyYAML resolves it as a float. A directory named `1_0e+3` therefore passes despite the union contract. The pre-62fbb72 pattern covered this form through its decimal/exponent branch, so this is a regression.\n  FIX: Add a distinct YAML-1.1 exponent-only float alternative, e.g. `|[-+]?[0-9][0-9_]*(?:[eE][-+][0-9]+)`, and add `1_0e+3` and signed/exponent variants to the corpus and wrong-type fixtures.\nCOVERAGE: ['Static review of commit 62fbb72’s checker diff, final implicit-type code, Gate-C evidence scripts, audit disposition, status update, and change-manifest files.', 'Checked the requested false-positive/false-negative resolver boundary; no tests were run.']\nLIMITS: ['Read-only inspection only; evidence scripts and external YAML parsers were not executed.', 'Review was scoped to the final 62fbb72 change, as requested.']\n\n[exited with code 0]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6400d2ab-c000-4a50-8538-af543151ef4c","session_id":"9823c1f0-89f5-4638-9223-c6892607ac2b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"9823c1f0-89f5-4638-9223-c6892607ac2b","version":"2.1.270","gitBranch":"main"}
